        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Previous research has indicated that the structure of the cellulose ribbon biosynthesized by <I>Acetobacter</I> during a flight of the NASA Reduced Gravity Laboratory was altered.  NASA’s Reduced Gravity Laboratory is a modified KC-135 aircraft designed to produce 20 seconds of microgravity from the top of a parabolic dive.  However, due to the limitations of reduced gravity research conducted at or near the surface of the Earth, it was not possible to unequivocally correlate these alterations with hypogravity.  This ground-based investigation explored different cultural approaches of <I>Acetobacter</I> that might be used in the Biological Research in a Canister (BRIC) system developed for space flight testing in the Shuttle middeck.
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
This experiment utilized the NASA Reduced Gravity Laboratory, a modified KC-135 aircraft designed to produce 20 seconds of microgravity during the top of a parabolic dive.  Approximately 40 parabolas were executed per mission (five missions total), and a period of 2 x g was integral to the pullout phase of each parabola.  
<P>
Two strains of <I>Acetobacter xylinum</I> were selected for this study, AY-201 (ATCC 23769) and NQ-5 (ATCC 53582).  Two BRIC units (120 mm in diameter and 312 mm in length) capable of holding nine standard size polycarbonate Petri dishes were used.  Cellulose biosynthesis was initiated on agar surfaces, liquid growth medium, and buffered glucose during parabolic flight and terminated with 2.0% sodium azide or 50.0% ethanol.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
While careful ground and inflight controls indicated normal, compact ribbons of microbial cellulose, data from five different flights consistently showed that during progression into the parabola (the 20 second microgravity phase, the polymorph normally is produced under strain, and the microgravity/hyergravity combination may relieve this stress to produce splayed ribbons.  An inflight video microscopy analysis of bacterial motions during a parabolic series demonstrated that the bacteria continue to synthesize cellulose during all phases of the parabolic series.  Thus, the splaying may be a reflection of a more subtle alteration, such as reduction of intermicrofibrillar hydrogen bonding.  Long-term microgravity exposures during spaceflight will be necessary to fully understand the cellulose alterations from the short-term microgravity experiments.<END>
        </div>

